      "page_title": "Large File Uploads",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q42. How do you handle large file uploads: multipart, presigned URLs or chunked?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Use ==presigned URLs== so the client uploads straight to object storage, not through your API.",
            "Multipart form suits small files up to a few MB",
            "Chunked or resumable upload survives flaky networks, enables retry",
            "Validate type and size, then scan after upload"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "PUT https://bucket.s3/key?",
            "  X-Amz-Signature=...",
            "Content-Type: image/png",
            "Expires: 15 min"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Client",
                "sub": "asks for URL",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "API",
                "sub": "signs presigned URL",
                "ink": "purple"
              },
              {
                "text": "Client",
                "sub": "PUT file to S3",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "API",
                "sub": "confirm and save",
                "ink": "orange"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Method",
            "Best for",
            "Watch out"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Multipart",
              "Small files, forms",
              "Ties up API server"
            ],
            [
              "Presigned URL",
              "Large files, direct",
              "Short expiry needed"
            ],
            [
              "Chunked",
              "Huge files, mobile",
              "Track chunk order"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Upload safety",
          "text": "Keep presigned URLs short-lived, lock them to one key and content type, and scan files after upload."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Version in the URL, never break clients silently, and send big files straight to storage with presigned URLs."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "lightbulb",
      "subtitle": "Evolve and upload"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Gateways and Load Balancers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q43. What does an API gateway do?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "It is the single front door that routes client requests to backend services and applies shared policies.",
            "==Auth==, rate limits and TLS handled once at the edge",
            "**Routing**, request transforms and response caching",
            "Logging and metrics for every call in one place"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "Client",
                "ink": "blue"
              },
              {
                "text": "Gateway",
                "sub": "auth, limit, route",
                "ink": "red"
              },
              {
                "text": "Orders svc",
                "ink": "green"
              },
              {
                "text": "Users svc",
                "ink": "green"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q44. What is the difference between an API gateway and a load balancer?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A load balancer ==spreads traffic== across identical servers, while a gateway applies API-level policy and routes by path.",
            "LB works at L4/L7 and checks **health**",
            "Gateway knows keys, users, versions and quotas",
            "Most stacks use both: gateway in front of LBs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Aspect",
            "Load balancer",
            "API gateway"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Goal",
              "Spread load",
              "Manage APIs"
            ],
            [
              "Routes by",
              "IP, port, host",
              "Path, method, key"
            ],
            [
              "Auth / quotas",
              "No",
              "Yes"
            ],
            [
              "Targets",
              "Same service copies",
              "Many services"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Gateway pitfall",
          "text": "A gateway is a single point of failure: run several copies and keep business logic out of it."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "apigateway",
      "aside": "edge first!",
      "subtitle": "Edge, tests, observability"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "API Testing and Observability",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q45. How do you test an API: unit, integration and contract tests?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Test in layers: many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, and contract tests guarding each consumer.",
            "**Unit**: one handler or function, dependencies mocked",
            "**Integration**: real DB, real HTTP, full request path",
            "**Contract**: provider keeps the shape consumers expect"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "test('GET /users/1', async () => {",
            "  const r = await api.get('/users/1');",
            "  expect(r.status).toBe(200);",
            "  expect(r.body).toHaveProperty('id');",
            "});"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q46. How do you monitor an API: which metrics, logs and traces matter?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Watch the ==golden signals== through metrics for trends, logs for detail and traces for where time went.",
            "**Metrics**: latency p95/p99, error rate, traffic, saturation",
            "**Logs**: structured JSON with a request ID, no secrets",
            "**Traces**: follow one request across every service hop"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Pillar",
            "Answers",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Metrics",
              "Is it broken?",
              "5xx rate 4%"
            ],
            [
              "Logs",
              "What happened?",
              "`req_id=ab12 timeout`"
            ],
            [
              "Traces",
              "Where is it slow?",
              "DB span 900ms"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Alert on symptoms",
          "text": "Page on user-facing p99 latency and error rate, not on every CPU spike."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "Key takeaways",
          "text": "Gateway = policy and routing, LB = traffic spreading. Test in layers, and alert on p99 latency and error rate."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "apigateway",
      "subtitle": "Edge, tests, observability"
